WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.249
A production bug once deleted customer data.

00:00:03.249 --> 00:00:06.186
Recovery took backups, and a war room.

00:00:06.277 --> 00:00:11.759
RollbackIQ is a recovery engineer for
release regressions, built on GitLab.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:16.833
Its rule is simple: a green rollback job is
not success.

00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:18.528
Measured recovery is.

00:00:18.944 --> 00:00:22.382
The whole lifecycle is one GitLab pipeline.

00:00:22.382 --> 00:00:24.693
The build job publishes one image.

00:00:24.693 --> 00:00:29.546
The deploy job starts version one in a demo
staging environment, and measures

00:00:29.546 --> 00:00:32.286
a healthy baseline from real requests.

00:00:32.702 --> 00:00:36.976
Now watch it run. This is live output from
the same scripts the

00:00:36.976 --> 00:00:42.769
pipeline jobs use. Version two deploys with
an injected fault: the same image,

00:00:42.769 --> 00:00:47.100
with different configuration. One bad window
is not enough.

00:00:47.191 --> 00:00:51.453
Window one: latency near nine times the
baseline.

00:00:51.453 --> 00:00:57.281
Still no incident. Window two confirms it,
and the detector opens an incident

00:00:57.281 --> 00:00:59.058
with a rollback proposal.

00:00:59.393 --> 00:01:05.292
A proposal changes nothing. A named person
must approve that exact proposal, by

00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:11.005
its hash. Then the safety gate runs eighteen
checks: the environment, the target

00:01:11.005 --> 00:01:13.873
release, the approver, the idempotency key.

00:01:13.964 --> 00:01:17.425
If one check fails, the rollback is refused.

00:01:17.737 --> 00:01:21.965
Here the gate passes, and the rollback puts
version one back.

00:01:21.965 --> 00:01:24.391
But a green job proves nothing.

00:01:24.391 --> 00:01:28.699
So a separate step measures a new window
that starts after the rollback:

00:01:28.699 --> 00:01:31.369
two hundred requests, over six seconds.

00:01:31.402 --> 00:01:35.049
Latency is back at the baseline, with zero
errors.

00:01:35.082 --> 00:01:37.917
Recovered, from independent evidence.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.549
Then a custom GitLab Duo flow investigates
the incident.

00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:47.797
It is defined in this repository, registered
in the AI Catalog, and started

00:01:47.797 --> 00:01:49.875
by a mention on the incident issue.

00:01:49.966 --> 00:01:53.474
It has no tool that can deploy or approve.

00:01:53.565 --> 00:01:55.610
Its note was not flattering.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:59.476
It found three values in our incident record
that did not match the

00:01:59.476 --> 00:02:03.052
artifacts, and it recommended hold and
escalate.

00:02:03.052 --> 00:02:04.782
We corrected the record.

00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:10.447
In GitLab, the same lifecycle ran on shared
runners, and recovery was verified

00:02:10.447 --> 00:02:14.360
there too. On one host we repeated it five
times.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:20.141
Five of five runs recovered, and five
healthy controls gave zero false positives.

00:02:20.476 --> 00:02:25.308
RollbackIQ does not restore deleted data,
and it says so.

00:02:25.341 --> 00:02:30.834
It makes a rollback a decision with
evidence: detected by measurement, approved
by

00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:34.259
a person, verified independently. Detect.

00:02:34.259 --> 00:02:36.059
Decide. Recover.

